10. Manual collection sweeps

A manual sweep instructs the application to check the monitored sources immediately.

During a sweep, it loads the relevant X profile areas, scrolls through the visible timeline and extracts supported content.

The application then:

Normalizes the collected records.
Compares them against the local database.
Rejects duplicates.
Stores newly discovered findings.
Updates the Live Feed.
Applies configured search presets.

Manual sweeps are useful when actively monitoring an event or newly added account.

11. Automatic sweeps

Automatic sweeps can be enabled under System settings.

The default interval is approximately every 30 minutes, although the interval can be changed.

Automatic sweeps focus primarily on newer activity, such as:

New posts
Recent replies
Reposts
Newly visible comments

They run only while the desktop application is open.

12. Incremental historical archive

The Incremental Archive system is designed to gradually attempt deeper collection without requesting the maximum possible depth in a single operation.

It can progressively work through:

Older posts
Older replies
Additional followers
Additional following accounts

Each archive cycle performs one bounded task and then rotates to another task.

For one monitored account, a typical rotation might be:

Attempt to collect older posts and replies
Attempt to collect more followers
Attempt to collect more following accounts
Return to older posts and replies

With multiple monitored accounts, the application rotates across the accounts and collection categories.

This creates a gradually expanding local archive rather than repeatedly collecting only the newest material.

13. Progressive depth

The application tracks collection depth for each archive operation.

For example:

A profile may initially be collected using a small number of scroll passes.
A later archive cycle uses additional scroll passes.
Future cycles attempt to reach further down the timeline.
Previously collected results are discarded as duplicates.
Only newly discovered records are added.

The same approach applies to follower and following lists.

This does not guarantee a complete account history. X may limit how far back a timeline or network list can be loaded.

14. Deduplication

Collected findings are deduplicated before being saved.

The application uses identifiers and record details to avoid repeatedly storing the same:

Post
Reply
Repost
Comment
Follower
Following account

Deduplication allows the archive to grow over time without filling the database with repeated copies of the same records.

15. Local evidence preservation

The application stores its investigative dataset locally on the user’s computer.

That includes:

Monitored sources
Captured posts
Replies
Reposts
Comments
Search presets
Analyst notes
Followers
Following records
Archive progress
Application settings

This means the collected archive is not dependent on a cloud dashboard controlled by a third party.

It also means the operator is responsible for protecting the computer and stored investigation data.

16. JSON export

Search results and collected findings can be exported to JSON.

JSON is useful for:

Preserving structured evidence
Importing data into another application
Writing custom analysis scripts
Building entity graphs
Comparing archives
Creating case-management integrations

JSON exports can retain individual fields rather than reducing the result to a visual report.

17. PDF reports

Findings can also be exported as formatted PDF reports.

Reports can include:

Application branding
Source accounts
Post content
Timestamps
URLs
Content type
Search context
Investigative notes

PDF exports are useful for human-readable case files, briefings or handoff documentation.

A PDF export should still be treated as an investigative report, not automatic proof that every statement contained in a captured post is true.

18. CSV network export

Follower and following records can be exported as CSV.

This is useful for:

Spreadsheet analysis
Sorting and filtering
Comparing account networks
Importing into graph tools
Identifying repeated accounts
Building relationship matrices
19. Authenticated X browser session

The application provides a dedicated browser window for signing into X.

The general workflow is:

Open System.
Select Connect or Reopen X.
Sign into X normally.
Close the X window.
Run collection operations from the application.

The authenticated browser partition is then reused for profile viewing, thread viewing and collection.

This is more straightforward than manually copying session cookies into configuration fields.

20. Challenge and restriction handling

If X presents a login challenge, verification page or temporary restriction, the application is designed to stop rather than attempt to bypass the control.

The investigator may need to reopen the X session and complete any required action manually.

The tool does not guarantee uninterrupted access. X can change its interface, loading behavior, authentication requirements or rate controls at any time.

21. Desktop application architecture

The application is built with:

Electron
React
TypeScript
Vite

Electron provides the Windows desktop shell and authenticated browser environment.

React and TypeScript provide the application interface, feed management, search tools, presets and investigation workspaces.

The application can be run from source or compiled into a Windows installer.

22. Standard investigation workflow

A practical workflow would be:

Sign into a dedicated research account.
Add one or more target usernames.
Choose which content types to collect.
Run an initial sweep.
Review the Live Feed.
Open relevant posts in their real threads.
Attach analyst notes to important findings.
Create keyword presets for the investigation.
Extract followers and following.
Search the network results.
Enable automatic sweeps for new activity.
Enable Incremental Archive for gradual historical collection.
Export selected findings to JSON, CSV or PDF.
Preserve the exported files with case identifiers and hashes where appropriate.
23. What it does not guarantee

The application cannot guarantee:

A complete historical copy of every X profile
Every reply in every conversation
Every follower or following account
Access to protected or restricted content
Access to deleted posts
Continuous collection while the application is closed
Permanent compatibility with future X interface changes
That collected statements are factually accurate
That an X account belongs to the person it claims to represent

Collected material should be corroborated before being used as an evidentiary conclusion.

